5ef84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272148 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272148 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140719" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272148 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272148 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272148 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f235e8a4-87f8-5bc9-a7bf-ca7b62641c4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272148 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}_{2}+2(0)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272149 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272149 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140720" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272149 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272149 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272149 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3c0726d-3efe-576b-8418-4bde00e5fffc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272149 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is an upper triangular matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=4,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{7}{1}=7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321172 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321172 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321172 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "027ec94f-19b6-5023-a751-ea66e6c774cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321225 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321225 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321225 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a29aa16-6c63-5a61-91a9-1f74b2ed3f26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921