0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{2}{5}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
